  "title": "A Minimal-Surface Counterexample to a Seshadri Lower Bound",
  "description": "<p>For every integer d at least 7, a classical integral plane pencil followed by a double base change gives a smooth minimal complex projective surface S with ample canonical bundle, K_S^2=6(d-1)(d-3), and an ample integral line bundle L with L^2=6 and epsilon(S,L;x) at most 1/(d-1). This strictly contradicts the proposed bound 1/(2+|K_S^2|^(1/4)) in Szemberg's 2007/2008 question, repeated as OWR21/2009 Conjecture2 and Primer-v2 Question6.1.6. The d=7 witness has K_S^2=144, polarization degree1 on the exhibited curve, and point multiplicity6. The proof supplies finite-degree pencil existence, all-curve ampleness, smoothness, connectedness, minimality and the degree-one lifted singular fibre. The additive2 is outside the fourth root, correcting a catalogue transcription.</p><p>Miranda's pencil and polarization are credited through Ein-Lazarsfeld1993, Bauer1999 and Bauer-Szemberg2010 Proposition2.1; the cover and positivity ingredients are classical. Two independent primary-source priority audits found no explicit earlier exact resolution in inspected text, but priority cannot be fully cleared. The full Bansal-Majumder2025 survey, Roy2019 thesis and other thesis bodies were not inspected; longer sources and citation bodies were selectively read and final journal versions were not globally compared. No firstness, new-method or verified continued-open-status claim is made. Only an upper Seshadri bound at a special point on varying surfaces is asserted, not an exact value, fixed-surface vanishing, rank-one or very-general-point violation. The authored-only package preserves18 original inputs, independent derivations and source receipts, four review families, pinned inventories, exact symbolic controls and a nine-job offline replay driver. SymPy1.14.0 and mpmath1.3.0 must be preinstalled. Raw third-party documents and account data are excluded. Finite controls supplement the geometric proof; integrity is not authenticity or priority.</p><p>AI tools were used extensively in solving, research, writing, source comparison, reproduction and adversarial verification. This is an unrefereed preprint with internal AI adversarial reviews; it has not received human peer review or proof-assistant certification.</p>",
